WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.500
The altimeter was spinning. I remember that clearly
not because it was unusual,

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:14.000
but because of how slowly it moved, like it had
something important to say.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Outside the cockpit there was nothing. No horizon.
No lights below. Just grey pressing in from every direction.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Tower had gone silent twenty minutes earlier.
My co-pilot, Marcus, was working the radios

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
but getting nothing back just static and the sound
of rain hammering against the fuselage.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.000
We were somewhere over the North Atlantic.
Eleven thousand feet, according to the instruments.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.000
I wasn't sure I believed them anymore.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:06.000
I looked at Marcus. He looked at me.
Neither of us said a word for ten seconds.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.000
That silence was the loudest thing I have ever
heard inside a cockpit.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Then he said, quietly, "What do you want to do?"
And I realised no one was going to answer that except me.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:36.000
I had trained for instrument failure. Every pilot does.
But training happens in a simulator,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
in a building, with an instructor who already knows the answer.
This was different.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:56.000
This was seven tonnes of metal and forty-two passengers,
and the only person who could bring them home

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
was the one holding the yoke.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:12.000
I went back to basics. Attitude. Altitude. Airspeed.
The three things your body wants to abandon

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:20.000
when it can't see and the instruments are lying.
I held the wings level by feel and started to climb,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:28.000
slowly, because height is time and time is options.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Marcus got a partial signal on the secondary radio 
just enough to get a bearing.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Forty minutes later we broke through the clouds.
There were lights below.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:02.000
I have landed that aircraft more than a thousand times.
I have never felt the wheels touch down the way I felt them that night.
